00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.710
I'm Indranil Chakraborty.

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:17.230
I'm the product guy with
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.840
And together with my colleagues
here, in this session

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.610
we're going to
cover three things.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.930
One is the key
challenges for Edge.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.350
And Google Cloud's offering
to address those challenges.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.210
Second is, how are we, as Google
Cloud and Google in general,

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.560
thinking about offering
an integrated AI platform.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.060
And finally, we'll have
some demos for you.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.040
So let's get started.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.990
So let's start with a
simple dictionary definition

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.660
of Internet of Things.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.120
By the way, the computer
is not working here.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.760
The Internet of Things, if
you look at the dictionary,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.520
the definition is
interconnection

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.480
of computing devices
embedded in everyday things

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:04.769
via the internet.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.020
Essentially, it
is everyday things

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.930
connected to the internet and,
hence, Internet of Things.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.980
And in this path
of interconnection,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.130
there are really three
interacting units.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:20.670
There's a network
of objects that

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.852
connect wired or
wirelessly to internet,

00:01:24.852 --> 00:01:26.310
and the network of
objects could be

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.990
devices, could be appliances,
cars, and so on and so forth.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.660
Then, there are gateways
with access bridges

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.870
between these devices
and the internet.

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:40.732
And finally, Cloud,
where you want

00:01:40.732 --> 00:01:42.690
to store the information
which you're receiving

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:47.370
from the devices and gateways
for processing it, and then

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:50.910
analyzing it to
derive intelligence.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.625
As you all might
already know, that it

00:01:53.625 --> 00:01:58.830
is estimated that by end of
2020 there'll be around $25

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.640
billion such install IT units.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.060
And these IT units
would be installed

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.729
across the consumer segments as
well as the business segments.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.730
So that's an enormous
number of devices

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.440
connected to the internet.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.220
And we are already generating
more data than ever

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.680
with these Internet
of Thing devices,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:26.510
and the data generation
is continuing to grow.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:30.280
So when Google--
around last year,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.610
we announced Cloud IoT
Core to really help

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:36.430
customers, enterprise
customers and developers,

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.250
to manage this massive
number of devices

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.920
and be able to
easily ingest influx

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.630
of data from these devices.

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:48.010
So Clout IoT Core is a
fully managed service

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.290
which allows you to
securely connect and manage

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:52.870
your devices on Google Cloud.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.240
And since our
announcements last year,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.820
the market reaction,
the reaction

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:02.370
from our customers and partner
has been really positive.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.830
It has been phenomenal.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:07.320
We have customers
in manufacturing,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.960
in a Smart Parking management,
health care, oil and gas

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.070
services who are connecting
their assets on the field

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.440
to Google Cloud IoT
Core so that they

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.770
can connect to all these
devices securely and then

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:26.590
use the rest of our Google
Cloud Analytics and AI services

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:31.180
for ingesting storing data and
really deriving intelligence.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.200
And as we started working
with Cloud IoT core,

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:38.990
our focus has been on
four critical areas.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.410
One is intelligence.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:44.080
So once you've collected all
this data from the installed

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:45.910
units, what do you
do with those data?

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:49.030
So we want to make sure
that we make it really easy

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:51.370
for you guys, for
customers and partners,

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:55.060
to derive intelligence from
the data that you collect.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.590
Second is scale.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.140
So Clout IoT Platform
and Cloud IoT Core

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:03.400
is a serverless service.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.620
So whether you
connect one device,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.690
or you connect
hundreds of thousands,

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.020
or even millions of devices,
you just connect your devices,

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.790
and we scale internally.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:14.350
As a customer or
as a developer, you

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.990
don't have to worry about
adding new infrastructure

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.750
or adding a new VM instance
as you add more devices.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:23.440
So we really have
focused on scale.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:25.390
Third is security.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.740
We wanted to make sure
that each IoT device there

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.750
can be individually
authenticated,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:36.010
and it makes a secure
connection to Google Cloud.

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:39.940
And finally, we focused on
building up a robust partner

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:40.490
ecosystem.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:42.880
So we've been working
really hard on these four

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:44.440
critical areas.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.500
And we identified
early on that there

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:52.840
is an emergent need for
running local services close

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.920
to the devices itself, to
the Edge devices itself.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.860
And in fact, all these
four critical areas

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:03.400
are relevant for
edge devices as well.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:09.040
And most important of those four
was intelligence at the edge.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.590
So lets take some
example use cases.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:15.520
And if you have a camera
pointed at your parking lot,

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.390
where you're using the camera
to count real time the number

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:24.612
of cars that are parked
in the parking lot,

00:05:24.612 --> 00:05:27.070
it's not really cost effective,
and it doesn't really makes

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:30.430
any sense to send every
frame of the video

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:33.052
from the camera to Cloud
to analyze and count

00:05:33.052 --> 00:05:35.260
the number of cars that are
there in the parking lot.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:39.120
What if you could just do
it in the camera itself?

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.020
If you have a
factory with robots,

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.750
they could be
regulatory reasons.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.480
There could be reasons for which
you want to protect your IP,

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:48.850
and you don't want to send
all the data to Google Cloud.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.760
You might want to filter some
of the data on the edge itself.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.900
If you're doing
object detection using

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.960
a camera or some other
device, and there are,

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.180
for privacy reasons,
you may not want

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.910
to send all of the
sensitive data to Cloud

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.620
but you would want to run
some of those analyses

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.790
and intelligence on
the device itself.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:19.860
So based on these, for
all these reasons before,

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.120
I'm very excited that we
announced two new products

00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:27.120
for Edge computing
and Edge intelligence.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.920
One is Cloud IoT Edge.

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.130
Cloud IoT Edge is
a software stack

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:35.730
which extends the AI and
data processing capabilities

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:38.620
of Google Cloud to the Edge.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:42.720
And second is Edge TPU,
which is a Google-designed

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.130
high-performing hardware chip to
run machine learning inference

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.390
on the Edge device itself.

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:51.990
So let's get a little bit more
into the details of these two

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:52.489
products.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:57.710
So with Cloud IoT
Edge, as I mentioned,

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.680
it's a software runtime that
you can run in your IoT gateway

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.450
devices.

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:06.370
You can run it on a camera.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:09.760
You can run it on any
connected device that

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:13.660
has some compute capabilities,
essentially a device that

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.760
has a CPU.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.220
And Cloud IoT Edge
software runtime

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.910
allows you to run machine
learning inference on the Edge

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:23.920
device itself.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:26.586
It has built-in
support for Edge TPU,

00:07:26.586 --> 00:07:30.610
so that you can have lightning
fast machine learning inference

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:33.180
on your device itself.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.000
With Cloud IoT Edge, you
can also locally store

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:38.260
some of your data.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:39.520
You can process it.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.200
You can do translation.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:43.540
You can do some
aggregation and filtration

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:47.450
before you send the
data back to Cloud

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:50.300
Android Things is one
of our, Google's, manage

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.670
operating system
for IoT devices,

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:53.840
which has got a secure boot.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:08:00.530
So we have designed from ground
up for security and software

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:01.350
update.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.720
And Cloud IoT Edge
runs on Android Things,

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.550
and we are planning to soon
have support for Linux versions

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:11.870
as well.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:15.740
And it securely connects your
Edge devices to Google Cloud.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.830
At the same time, we've
also added enhanced security

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:22.700
through hardware root of trust,
and Cloud IoT Edge seamlessly

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:24.860
works with Google
Cloud IoT Core and rest

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:28.180
of Google Cloud IoT Platform
for hassle free provisioning.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:29.810
So it's a complete
software stack

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.480
to run machine learning
inference on the Edge device

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:34.460
itself.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:38.450
And there are two
key components that

00:08:38.450 --> 00:08:41.500
enables Cloud IoT Edge to--

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:46.220
for real time AI, on
the edge device itself.

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:49.460
There's a TensorFlow
Lite based runtime

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.130
that allows you to run those
machine learning inference

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.890
on the Edge, reducing
latency and then increasing

00:08:54.890 --> 00:08:57.300
the versatility of
your Edge device.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.230
And because we use TensorFlow
Lite runtime, which

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:06.160
is an open source, you
can use Cloud IoT Edge

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.710
with Edge TPU for
really lightning

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.730
fast and high-performing
performance.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:15.060
But if you have a device which
doesn't have an Edge TPU,

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.370
or your use case doesn't require
the horsepower of an Edge TPU

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:21.890
chip, you can still
run Cloud IoT Edge

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:26.270
and do local inference on
your device, which has a GPU

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:29.910
or just has a CPU itself, so
it's pretty flexible that way.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:31.820
And the second
component is IoT Core

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:37.110
that securely connects your
Edge device to Google Cloud.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:41.270
So you can manage your software.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:44.210
You can do software
and firmware updates.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.550
You can exchange data
and also exchange

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:51.050
machine learning models between
your Edge device and the Cloud.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:54.650
So combined with these two
key components, what Cloud IoT

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:58.940
Edge offers you is the software
runtime for running machine

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.570
learning inference, but also
to orchestrate your models

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:06.274
and data exchange
with Google Cloud.

00:10:06.274 --> 00:10:07.190
Let's take an example.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:12.110
Say you have a factory with a
high velocity assembly line,

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:15.530
and you want to detect
anomalies real time.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:19.760
And you have a camera that
has our Edge TPU chip,

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.960
and it's running Android Things,
and it has our Cloud IoT Edge

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:26.640
software runtime running on it.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:30.710
And you have collected
historical data

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.230
from your assembly line
production, which you can then

00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:36.050
use which using Cloud
Machine Learning

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:40.580
Engine you can train a model
to accurately detect defects.

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.500
You can then push
down that model

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:45.920
to the camera via our Cloud
IoT Core and Cloud IoT Edge

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:47.120
software.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.300
And using Edge
TPU, now you can do

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:54.620
real time detection of anomaly
on your high velocity assembly.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:58.040
So with Cloud IoT edge as
a software, and Edge TPU

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:00.710
chip as our hardware
chip, together

00:11:00.710 --> 00:11:03.620
with our Cloud
platform, you can really

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:09.800
build an integrated AI solution
with our Cloud IT platform.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:11.960
Now, let's look at Edge TPU.

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:15.050
And to tell you
more about Edge TPU,

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:18.470
and the motivations for
building Edge TPU chip,

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.440
let me invite my
friend, Olivier Temam,

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:24.910
who is the tech lead for Edge
TPU in the Google AI team.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:25.410
Olivier.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:33.230
OLIVIER TEMAM: Thanks, Indranil.

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:34.590
So good morning, everyone.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.680
It's a real pleasure to
be here with you today

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:39.050
to talk about Edge TPU.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.570
And actually, what we
want to do is not only

00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:45.500
to present you what
Edge TPU is, but we also

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:49.780
want to explain why we have
developed Edge TPU Google.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:51.290
And to do that, we
want to walk you

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:54.990
through the four key motivations
that you see on the slide.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.620
So the first motivation
that we had is we

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:00.600
want to bring high-quality
AI to the Edge.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:05.190
And by high-quality I mean
high speed and high accuracy.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.150
So at Google we are
developing machine

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.050
learning models for Edge.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:12.350
One of our flagship models
for image classification

00:12:12.350 --> 00:12:13.850
is MobileNet.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.160
And it's not a unique
feature, but one

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:18.530
of the nice things
about MobileNet

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:21.050
is that it's a highly
configurable model,

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:25.380
and you can easily trade more
computations for more accuracy.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.550
So just to get an idea of the
relationship between the two,

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:32.690
let's look at this graph,
where you see on the x-axis

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.520
the computational
cost of running

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:37.430
one inference at
the Edge expressed

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:39.500
in millions of multiply-adds.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.600
And on the y-axis,
you have the accuracy.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.980
So if you're shooting for a
moderate amount of accuracy,

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:50.510
like 60%, you're going to
pay 60 million multiply-adds.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:53.150
That's 120 million operations.

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:56.960
But if you want to bump
that up to 75% accuracy,

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:00.740
you're going to have to
pay 10 times that cost, 1.2

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:02.120
billion operations.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.840
And that particular graph stops
at 600 million multiply-adds.

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:10.050
You can invest more computations
to get even more accuracy.

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:13.670
So the point here is that
the computational cost

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:16.560
of high accuracy is very steep.

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.770
And what we want to
do there in order

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:21.800
to sustain high-quality
AI at the Edge

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:24.320
is to deliver high performance.

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.200
But because this is also
about embedded applications,

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:29.480
we have to do this
at a very low cost

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:32.840
and, naturally, in a small
physical and power footprint.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:34.940
And that was basically
the first charter

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:37.070
that we had with Edge TP.

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:38.990
So the second
motivation that we had

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:41.780
relates to what
Indranil just explained.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:46.040
We are building an end-to-an
AI infrastructure, Cloud

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.890
to Edge, AI to hardware,
and the key goal

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.920
is to make it as easy as
possible for our customers

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.370
to deploy their AI-based
solutions by piggybacking,

00:13:55.370 --> 00:13:57.200
basically, this infrastructure.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.560
So it involves work
across the three stocks--

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:02.300
AI, software, and hardware,
naturally-- and as you

00:14:02.300 --> 00:14:05.810
all know, we have already been
developing a custom AI hardware

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:08.150
solution for the
Cloud, Cloud TPU.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:11.570
So by introducing Edge TPU,
the other thing we wanted to do

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:15.330
is to complement that
infrastructure, so again,

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.870
to make it as easy as
possible for our customers

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.670
to deploy their solution.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:22.190
Now we think,
actually, Edge TPU is

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:24.830
going to bring a lot of value
to our customers in terms

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:27.560
of helping them
deploy AI at the edge,

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:31.010
but we also are developing
an open infrastructure

00:14:31.010 --> 00:14:34.250
so we see Edge TPU
as only one option

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:36.470
that we are making
available to our customers.

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:39.590
And customers remain completely
free to choose any hardware

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:41.840
solution they want.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.230
So the third motivation that
we had is more technical,

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:48.350
and it relates, actually, to
a fairly fundamental point

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.944
about custom hardware
design, which is actually

00:14:50.944 --> 00:14:51.860
very often overlooked.

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:54.800
It's a simple one, but
it's very often overlooked.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.200
And it's the fact that
custom hardware design

00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:59.840
is all about
leveraging in hardware

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.810
the properties of the
algorithms, so in this case

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:04.250
AI algorithms.

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:07.460
So that can be a challenge
for hardware designers

00:15:07.460 --> 00:15:09.650
because it means
they have to develop

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:13.820
a fairly intimate knowledge
of the target algorithms of AI

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.380
algorithms.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:18.540
So that can be a challenge
for most organizations,

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:21.050
but for Google it can
also be an opportunity

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:23.690
because all hardware
designers have access

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:26.216
to abundant AI expertise.

00:15:26.216 --> 00:15:27.590
And therefore,
they can know what

00:15:27.590 --> 00:15:29.660
the latest trends
in AI, and that

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:32.090
can help them decide
what kind of features

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:36.120
they need to bring within
their hardware designs.

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:39.800
Now, that's one advantage of
having both expertise, hardware

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:43.130
and AI, within the same company,
but it's not the only one.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:46.010
What we can do, and what
we're increasingly doing,

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:49.100
is that we can also go the
other way around and start

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.620
adopting AI algorithms
so that they work

00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:55.940
as well as possible
under custom AI hardware.

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:58.940
And the last point is that
probably one of the biggest

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.640
misconceptions about
custom AI hardware

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.380
is that it's
primarily a hardware

00:16:03.380 --> 00:16:07.600
task while, in fact, the biggest
difficulty is the software

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:11.570
layer, which you use to map
AI algorithms onto the custom

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:12.690
hardware.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:14.480
So what we are
really striving to do

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:17.440
is to develop a holistic
approach across the three

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.670
stack, and in both
as a conviction,

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:23.430
but increasingly based
on our experience,

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:26.090
this is what is going to yield
the best results in terms

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:28.800
of custom AI hardware design.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:31.340
Our fourth motivation
relates to this one,

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:33.330
to the one I just presented.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:35.940
So it's not only that all
hardware designers have

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:38.400
to have access to the
latest trends in AI,

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:42.270
but it's also that AI is
evolving extremely quickly.

00:16:42.270 --> 00:16:45.210
This graph that you see here
has been collected by colleagues

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:48.990
at Google AI, and it shows the
number of AI-related research

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.750
articles which have been
submitted to archive

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:53.190
over the past decades.

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:55.860
And the key message
here is that this curve

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:57.900
has an exponential,
basically, shape,

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:02.190
so there is just underlines the
very rapid progression of AI.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.815
So that, again, can be both a
challenge and an opportunity.

00:17:05.815 --> 00:17:07.440
It is a challenge
because, as you know,

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.609
the timeline of hardware is
very different from the timeline

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:14.819
of AI research, but it
can become an opportunity

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:18.540
if we learn to tackle in
hardware this rapid evolution

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:19.440
of AI.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:21.900
If we do that, then
what is going to happen

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.960
is that the performance
of the custom AI hardware,

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.569
again expressed in
speed and accuracy,

00:17:27.569 --> 00:17:31.200
is now going to progress more
or less at the same pace as that

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:32.430
of AI algorithms.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:34.570
And as you can see,
that's very fast.

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:38.070
So one way to think about
it is that, increasingly,

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:42.390
AI algorithms are playing for
custom AI hardware more or less

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:44.930
the same role as
transistor technology

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:47.760
it's playing for general
purpose architectures.

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:51.240
And when we sat down
to design Edge TPU,

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:53.610
from the beginning we had
that objective in mind,

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:55.860
which is, how are we
going to tackle this very

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:57.600
rapid evolution of AI?

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.250
And what we've
designed is actually--

00:17:59.250 --> 00:18:01.710
what we've created is
not just one design,

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:05.650
but it is a design approach
exactly for that propose.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:09.000
That's also why, when we
think about the first chip

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:12.540
that we are presenting today,
even though it's achieving

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:16.020
a very good performance per
dollar ratio, which we think

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:19.720
is going to help disseminate
high-quality AI at the Edge,

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:22.500
we also think about it
only as a first step

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:24.180
in a long journey,
which is going

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:26.100
to be largely driven by AI.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.320
And we are already
well underway.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.350
We have our second design,
and we've already started

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.405
to work on the third design.

00:18:33.405 --> 00:18:36.570
And at every step, what
we're going to do basically

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:41.250
is to improve the AI support,
improve performance and power,

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:43.750
and bring more functionalities.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:47.670
So those are some of the
key motivations that we had

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:49.860
actually to design Edge TPU.

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.740
And now to present you
in more details what

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:55.890
we are going to provide to our
customers in the coming months,

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:59.790
I'm going to invite Ajay, who
is the Edge TPU product manager

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:02.080
to present you that.

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:02.580
Thank you.

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:11.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:13.300
AJAY K. NAIR: Thanks, Olivier.

00:19:13.300 --> 00:19:14.980
Good morning, everyone.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:18.210
I'm Ajay Nair, the product
manager for Edge TPUs.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.370
We'll go over quickly
on what we're releasing

00:19:21.370 --> 00:19:25.000
and how users can have access
to what's being announced today.

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:30.790
So that is the Edge TPU.

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:33.940
One thing to note here is that
the one cent that is shown

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:36.700
is indicative of the
size of the Edge TPU.

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:39.318
It does not indicate the cost.

00:19:39.318 --> 00:19:41.758
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:41.758 --> 00:19:44.200
Aw.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:47.420
But in all seriousness, though,
when we were designing the Edge

00:19:47.420 --> 00:19:52.180
TPUs, we were striving to
achieve very high performance

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:56.470
per dollar, and I'm
proud to say that we've

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:58.040
been able to achieve that.

00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.560
Our goals have
been met, and we've

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:03.190
been able to do that at
a relatively low power

00:20:03.190 --> 00:20:05.920
footprint and also
physical footprint

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:09.280
on how big the Edge TPU is.

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.830
And a combination of
the high performance

00:20:11.830 --> 00:20:16.420
per dollar, low power, as well
as the low physical footprint

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:20.710
allows broad deployment of Edge
TPUs in multiple applications.

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:24.220
And it helps our goal
to democratize AI.

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:27.290
In terms of the
Edge TPUs itself,

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:29.290
the first generation that
is being announced now

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:32.470
and the roadmap is an
inference accelerator,

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:35.410
and the architecture
was optimized

00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:36.829
for vision applications.

00:20:36.829 --> 00:20:38.120
Of course, it's an accelerator.

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:40.060
It can be used for
multiple models,

00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.470
but it was optimized for vision.

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:45.080
It can run concurrent
state of-- and what

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:47.130
we can do with an
accelerator at the Edge

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:51.160
is essentially run multiple
state of the art models

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:54.700
concurrently, for example,
on a high resolution video

00:20:54.700 --> 00:20:56.510
without losing frame rate.

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:57.670
Right?

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.970
As an example of what
Olivier mentioned earlier,

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:04.090
the MobileNet V2, we can achieve
several hundred inferences

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:08.080
per second on the
highest accuracy

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:09.430
versions of those models.

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:18.160
So what we are announcing is
not just the hardware, though.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.770
What we are trying to do
is bring the leadership

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:25.820
and the expertise that Google
has in hardware, software,

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:30.170
and AI, and combine those
into a solution which

00:21:30.170 --> 00:21:32.160
customers can have access to.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.110
So as Indranil
pointed out earlier,

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:37.850
we already talked about the
Cloud IoT edge, and the Edge

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:39.710
TPU, and how they interact.

00:21:39.710 --> 00:21:43.760
We are adding components which
make use of our expertise in AI

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:45.360
as well as software.

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.510
So in AI, for
example, we will be

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:50.060
releasing at the time of
the product launch, which

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:53.030
is in October when early access
customers can have access

00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:59.040
to this product, a set
of precompiled models.

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:03.440
These are going to be compiled
to TensorFlow Lite, which

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:06.780
is Google's open source
AI framework for the Edge.

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:10.070
And TensorFlow Lite
allows low latency

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:13.860
at small binary size for
your Edge applications.

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:16.470
We'll be announcing
models, for example,

00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:19.970
that are MobileNet based
models and other architectures

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:20.900
as well.

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:23.630
And it will also be
integrated with ML Kit.

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:25.820
ML Kit is a beta product
that Google announced

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:31.310
a few months ago,
which has an SDK, which

00:22:31.310 --> 00:22:34.500
allows users to have
commonly used AI

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:36.750
applications at the Edge.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:39.820
And if you have
more complex needs,

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:43.250
ML Kit also provides
an API for you

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:45.825
to serve those models
onto your Edge devices.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:51.650
The next step after
that is we would

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:55.014
enable transfer learning
on those precompiled models

00:22:55.014 --> 00:22:56.430
that we are going
to be providing.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:57.840
So what is transfer learning?

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:00.150
Transfer learning
is a method where

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:04.140
you can repurpose an existing
model which is made for one use

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:07.280
case and customize that
based on your own data

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:10.410
sets for your specific use case.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.780
So that's a very
powerful method,

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:16.800
and it really helps because
the investment needed

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:20.250
from users in terms of the time,
the effort, and the expertise

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.910
in machine learning
is vastly reduced.

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.890
So the existing precompiled
models can be repurposed.

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:30.690
You can bring your own
data and reuse your data

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:34.470
for your specific
application needs.

00:23:34.470 --> 00:23:39.680
In a future update, we will
also be releasing the TensorFlow

00:23:39.680 --> 00:23:45.150
light compiler for Edge TPUs
so you can start your model

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.090
design from scratch.

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:51.700
We also want to make
it easy for customers

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:56.470
to deploy Edge TPU at your
applications, the idea being we

00:23:56.470 --> 00:23:59.410
would like to be able to
envision your use cases that

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:01.330
serve your business needs.

00:24:01.330 --> 00:24:04.630
And using the products that
we're going to be releasing,

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:09.550
you can iterate, and prototype,
use the models in your hardware

00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:13.150
applications, use the
transfer learning function

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:15.520
to optimize those
models for your use case

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.530
and test it out in your system.

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:19.780
And when you're satisfied
with the results,

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:22.360
you deploy that in
your real applications.

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:27.010
And we're also announcing and
making available the Edge TPU

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:29.860
development kit, and there are
two versions of the Development

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:32.470
Kit, depending on where
you are in the ecosystem.

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:36.880
If you want a fully integrated
solution with the Edge

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:39.910
TPU connected to an SoC,
what we are announcing

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:42.399
is a system on module.

00:24:42.399 --> 00:24:44.190
So what you see here
is a system on module.

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:50.110
It has the Edge TPU
ASIC connects to a CPU

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:53.290
and also a crypto for security.

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:57.770
That module connects onto--

00:24:57.770 --> 00:24:59.500
I'll take a break there.

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:02.900
The Edge TPU is that red square
that you are seeing there.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:05.740
And this is to scale,
by the way in terms

00:25:05.740 --> 00:25:09.880
of the famous one cent.

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:13.030
The Edge TPU SoM
connects to a baseboard,

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:15.400
and the baseboard
essentially expands

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.070
the I/O connectivity
options that are available.

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:21.160
So there's a whole
slew of connectivity

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:24.610
there that you have
USB-A ports, USB-C, HDMI.

00:25:24.610 --> 00:25:27.340
There's a MIPI interface to
connect your camera onto it.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:33.220
It's got a WiFi interface, an
ethernet, and a five volt DC

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:36.800
power to power the devices.

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:38.870
So if you need a fully
integrated solution,

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:40.030
this is what you use.

00:25:40.030 --> 00:25:44.620
The SOC will have Android
Things preloaded onto it.

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:46.266
It can connect
onto the internet.

00:25:46.266 --> 00:25:47.890
You can download the
precompiled models

00:25:47.890 --> 00:25:50.840
that we mentioned in
the previous slides,

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.110
and you can run
inference locally.

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:56.720
It also will have the
IoT Edge solution.

00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:58.420
So once you run
inference locally,

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:01.270
your application can decide
what to do with the local data

00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:07.150
and also use IoT Edge
to load back the data

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:10.540
as you need back to the Cloud
for storage or retrieval

00:26:10.540 --> 00:26:14.550
or for further analysis.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:18.520
We are also announcing
the Edge TPU accelerator.

00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:21.680
It's a USB-C form factor device.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:24.520
And if you have an
existing system that

00:26:24.520 --> 00:26:27.220
has a host interface
available, we

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:31.420
can just connect the Edge TPU
accelerator using the USB-C

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:33.940
onto your systems and
accelerate your machine

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:35.720
learning workloads.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:40.130
So both of these solutions
have been designed internally

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:43.180
at Google with partnership
from external vendors

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:45.010
for different components.

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:49.270
It's been designed
by the Google AIY

00:26:49.270 --> 00:26:54.790
team, which is a team within
the Google AI business function.

00:26:57.280 --> 00:26:57.780
All right.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:02.310
And to show some
functionality of the boards

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:05.850
and what we can do with it,
I'd like to invite Preston

