00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:19.190
on the far other side.

00:28:19.190 --> 00:28:24.699
So what we've got is we've
got two cameras set up.

00:28:24.699 --> 00:28:25.740
AJAY K. NAIR: [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:25.740 --> 00:28:28.730
PRESTON HOLMES: Yeah, just
your seats were in the frame.

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:32.120
So we've got two
cameras set up, and what

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:34.691
we're using are high resolution
cameras, high frame rate

00:28:34.691 --> 00:28:35.190
cameras.

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:39.150
These are 1080p cameras filming
at 60 frames per second.

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:43.460
And you can see already that if
you look at the top frame, that

00:28:43.460 --> 00:28:47.350
is a system without the
TPU, the frames per second

00:28:47.350 --> 00:28:50.080
in which we're able to
compute and analyze the video

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:53.180
are already down 13
frames per second.

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:55.270
Whereas the frames
per second with TPU

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.710
are still at a very high
frame rate processing

00:28:57.710 --> 00:28:58.710
of 45 frames per second.

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:00.293
Now, what I'm going
to do, I was going

00:29:00.293 --> 00:29:03.050
to invite my colleague, Matt,
one of the IoT engineers

00:29:03.050 --> 00:29:06.940
who helped us with
this demo, on stage.

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:09.290
And so you can see that,
as Matt walks onstage,

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:12.332
we've detected Matt.

00:29:12.332 --> 00:29:13.790
We see him onstage,
and you can see

00:29:13.790 --> 00:29:16.400
that already the frame
rates starting to drop

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.490
a little bit without the TPU.

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:22.560
Now, all of this
inference, all of the ML

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:25.520
that's detecting Matt on
stage is happening entirely

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:26.370
on the Edge.

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:29.060
So we're not able to
realistically stream

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.580
this kind of high frame
rate, high resolution video

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:33.200
directly to the
Cloud, so we need

00:29:33.200 --> 00:29:35.800
to be able to compute on
this information that's

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:38.540
so rich strictly on the Edge.

00:29:38.540 --> 00:29:40.790
But since we have
now some information

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:44.972
we're interested in, we
can detect essentially

00:29:44.972 --> 00:29:46.680
some information about
what we're seeing.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:48.096
So one of the
things is we can now

00:29:48.096 --> 00:29:50.450
look at just the simple
metric of how many things

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:53.210
have we detected that we're
interested in in the frame?

00:29:53.210 --> 00:29:56.960
And we may average this out
over five seconds locally

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:59.720
on the Edge so that we have a
simple metric that we can then

00:29:59.720 --> 00:30:00.570
send to the Cloud.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:02.570
And once we're sending
information in the Cloud,

00:30:02.570 --> 00:30:05.030
we can start to visualize
in things like a dashboard.

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:07.880
So I have, starting out
here, a very simple dashboard

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:13.730
that I can just see that we have
one person on stage right now.

00:30:13.730 --> 00:30:15.560
But as a few other
colleagues gradually

00:30:15.560 --> 00:30:20.270
join Matt and we
increase that number,

00:30:20.270 --> 00:30:22.959
we can start to look at
what that performance is.

00:30:22.959 --> 00:30:24.500
So in this case,
we're not only going

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.336
to send just the
count of people,

00:30:26.336 --> 00:30:27.710
but we're interested
in some more

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:30.050
information about
the performance

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:33.740
of the actual system here.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:37.580
So I'm going to switch briefly
to the dashboards for a moment

00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:41.730
so you can sort of see
what that looks like.

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:45.470
So here we're looking at
three pieces of information.

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:48.530
The top is the number
of objects detected.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:52.209
The second graph
is the frame rate,

00:30:52.209 --> 00:30:53.750
and then the third
is sort of a gauge

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:55.110
of the instantaneous frame rate.

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:58.370
And you can see
that, with Edge TPU,

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:01.080
we're still holding steady
at about 30 frames per second

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:02.090
of effective processing.

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:06.170
Now, this is not just one
model running per frame.

00:31:06.170 --> 00:31:10.320
We're actually running a
complex set of cascading models,

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:13.730
so we're doing many
inferences per frame

00:31:13.730 --> 00:31:17.070
to achieve the information
we're interested in.

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:21.500
And so as you can see, as
the number of people in frame

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:27.380
goes up, the inference really
starts to drop without a TPU.

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:29.490
And why does this matter?

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:31.160
Well, if you're
looking at something

00:31:31.160 --> 00:31:34.776
like this very high frame
rate, high resolution video,

00:31:34.776 --> 00:31:36.150
it lets us do a
couple of things.

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:43.820
One is it's letting us
detect that there are still

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:45.821
people who might be
very small in the frame.

00:31:45.821 --> 00:31:46.320
Right?

00:31:46.320 --> 00:31:49.550
So if you're thinking of looking
at, say, very small defects

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:53.510
on equipment, surface
defects, or small parts,

00:31:53.510 --> 00:31:58.010
high resolution allows you
to still provide the machine

00:31:58.010 --> 00:31:59.900
learning full information.

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:02.030
And we're not only
using high resolution

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:03.660
on the camera input side.

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:07.410
We're actually not extremely
downsampling that video

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:10.430
to feed to a model, so we're
able to feed high resolution

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:12.920
directly into the model
because of the computation

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:15.860
power of the Edge TPU.

00:32:15.860 --> 00:32:19.370
And as these numbers
increase, you'll

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:22.520
continue to see frame
rate dropping on the left,

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:26.630
but we want to be
able to also show

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:28.310
why does a frame rate matter?

00:32:28.310 --> 00:32:30.830
If we're losing frames,
what are we losing?

00:32:30.830 --> 00:32:33.170
So this model is not
only detecting things

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.270
like the person on
stage, but we're

00:32:35.270 --> 00:32:36.980
able to detect a
degree of information

00:32:36.980 --> 00:32:40.250
about what their orientation is,
what their attention is looking

00:32:40.250 --> 00:32:43.220
at, and we can count these
as essentially glances

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:44.510
at the camera.

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:48.720
Now, these are glances
that last only a moment.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:51.380
And so if we look
now at the way we've

00:32:51.380 --> 00:32:54.350
counted glances between
these two systems,

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:58.130
you can see that many glances
are simply missed by the system

00:32:58.130 --> 00:33:00.860
without a TPU because
they're so momentary.

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:02.450
And if you think
about applications

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:05.600
of the TPU in places such
as high-speed manufacturing

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:09.590
lines or other places where
momentary information that

00:33:09.590 --> 00:33:12.270
might exist in an acoustic
signal that will be

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:14.430
dropped or missed,
these essentially

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:19.590
act as dropping important
events in the system you're

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:21.060
trying to analyze.

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:24.750
And so as these systems
are unable to keep up,

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:27.120
you're essentially losing
critical information.

00:33:27.120 --> 00:33:30.090
And again, all that we're
interested in on the Cloud side

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:33.060
here is these
counts and metrics.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:36.810
We don't have a need to send
a complex or potentially

00:33:36.810 --> 00:33:39.240
sensitive information
that's happening on the Edge

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:40.950
directly to the
Cloud for processing.

00:33:40.950 --> 00:33:46.080
We can keep that processing
happening strictly on the Edge.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:50.160
So thank you very
much, colleagues.

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:54.646
And with that, I will return the
presentation back to Indranil.

